Adding a Domain Entry to the Windows Hosts File

To test your local virtual host on a Windows machine, you need to map the domain name
(e.g., example.com) to your local machine (usually 127.0.0.1/192.168.20.10) by editing the hosts file.

Follow these steps:

1. Open Notepad as Administrator:
   - Press the Start menu, search for "Notepad"
   - Right-click on Notepad and select "Run as administrator"

2. In Notepad, open the hosts file:
   File path: C:\Windows\System32\drivers\etc\hosts

   You might need to change the file type filter to "All Files (*.*)" to see it.

3. Add a new line at the end of the file like this:
   127.0.0.1   example.com
   127.0.0.1   www.example.com

   Replace "example.com" with your actual domain name.


4. Save the file.

5. Test it:
   - Open a browser and go to http://example.com
   - You should see your local site being served

Notes:
  - Each entry must start with 127.0.0.1 followed by the domain name
  - Make sure there are no typos or trailing characters
  - If it's not working, try flushing your DNS cache:
    Open Command Prompt and run: ipconfig /flushdns
